═══ THE FIX — ADR-0171 (Zak-Code PR #476): the fold keeps what the author EMPHASIZED ═══
  Lowercase "must" cannot be the signal — counted per section, the rule section scores 8.4 mandate words per KB
  against 7.8 ("Continuous integration") and 7.0 ("Concurrency"): a density threshold would be a fit to one file.
  What separates the rule is that its author emphasized it: MUST / NEVER in capitals (3 in the rule section, 0 in
  every filler section), the RFC-2119 convention; zak-code/CLAUDE.md bolds its non-negotiables the same way. So
  _fit_sections gets a middle tier: heading names a mandate (0) → body carries an EMPHASIZED mandate — capitals
  MUST / MUST NOT / NEVER / ALWAYS / MANDATORY / REQUIRED / FORBIDDEN / PROHIBITED / DO NOT / SHALL, or the same
  words in **bold** / __underscores__ (1) → the rest in document order (2). The 14o / 14u / 13 / 14x folds keep the
  same sections (only the note's wording changes); the Mind's 47K CLAUDE.md gains the four sections that say MUST
  NOT / NEVER. Tests: emphasized body kept whole past the cap; bold as emphasis; lowercase not promoted (omitted
  by name); tier order heading > body > plain. Full suite 3927 passed. Residual, stated: an unemphasized lowercase
  rule under a plain heading is lexically indistinguishable from guidance and still folds in document order.

